<!-- THE CONVENTIONAL ENTRY POINT: /sitemap.xml (#11615).
     Nothing served this path until now, so every crawler, SEO tool and Search
     Console "add sitemap" box that reaches for the conventional URL got a 404 —
     while robots.txt advertised four real sitemaps under names nobody probes.

     ONE DOCUMENT ON BOTH HOSTS, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS A RECORDED DEVIATION.
     The Sitemaps protocol (sitemaps.org, "Using Sitemap index files") says an
     index "can only specify Sitemaps that are found on the same site as the
     Sitemap index file … not on http://www.example.com or
     http://yourhost.yoursite.com" — a SUBDOMAIN is the spec's own counterexample,
     and this index is served from two of them while naming all four sitemaps.

     OVERRIDES: the Sitemaps protocol's same-site rule for index files — this
     project publishes ONE host-agnostic index from both premise-labs hosts
     instead of a per-host one, because the sitemaps are already cross-submitted
     from robots.txt on both hosts (the discovery path used since 2026-08-17) and
     a per-host index cannot be expressed by one static file. Google waives the
     same-site rule for cross-submitted sitemaps; a crawler that enforces it
     strictly still finds all four through robots.txt. Deviation recorded in
     `website/website_architecture.md` §6.5; a per-host index is #11624.

     The <loc> set below must equal the `Sitemap:` lines in robots.txt in BOTH
     directions — `test_the_sitemap_index_and_robots_advertise_the_same_set`
     holds the two files to each other, so neither can drift. blog/sitemap.xml
     is dynamic (published posts only); the other three are files in some tree. -->
<sitemapindex xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://premiselabs.co/sitemap-company.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://tortoise.premiselabs.co/sitemap-product.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://app.premiselabs.co/sitemap.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
  <sitemap>
    <loc>https://tortoise.premiselabs.co/blog/sitemap.xml</loc>
  </sitemap>
</sitemapindex>
